# KickCast backend .env - copy to .env. The site works with NO keys at all (openfootball,
# football-data.co.uk, StatsBomb, and the international results/goalscorers CSVs are all
# free and keyless). Every variable below unlocks something extra and is optional.

# Path to the SQLite database. Optional - defaults to data/kickcast.db.
# KICKCAST_DB_PATH=data/kickcast.db

# football-data.org free tier (free, no cost, 10 req/min). Would unlock Champions League
# fixtures/results, which we have no other free source for, and with it Champions League
# trophy odds. NOT YET IMPLEMENTED as of this commit - see MORNING_REPORT.md for why
# (real cross-league team-strength ratings are a separate research task, not just a key).
# FOOTBALL_DATA_ORG_API_KEY=

# Gemini API free tier (free, no cost). Unlocks natural-language understanding for the
# "Ask about soccer" assistant plus Google Search grounding for questions outside our
# database (match write-ups, news). Without this, the assistant runs in a DB-only mode:
# a small set of keyword-matched question shapes (next match / last result / table
# position / head-to-head prediction), never natural language understanding.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# Daily call budget before the assistant falls back to DB-only mode, to stay inside the
# free tier. Optional - defaults to 250.
# GEMINI_DAILY_QUOTA=250

# YouTube Data API v3 free tier (free, no cost, 10,000 quota units/day; search.list costs
# 100 units, so ~90 searches/day by default). Used ONLY to attach an official highlight
# video link to an answer already sourced from our database or search grounding - never
# as a source of facts itself. Without this, answers just have no video link attached.
# YOUTUBE_API_KEY=
# YOUTUBE_DAILY_QUOTA=90
# JSON map of channel name -> real YouTube channel ID (the UC... string from that
# channel's own "About" page, NOT the @handle), restricting video search to verified
# official channels. Left unset by default (we have not verified any real channel IDs
# ourselves - see kickcast_api/assistant/youtube.py). Without this, search still works
# but isn't restricted to a known-official channel.
# YOUTUBE_OFFICIAL_CHANNELS={"UEFA": "UCxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"}
